.TH GODELCTL 8 "2026-09-15" "Godel 0.9.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
godelctl \- control a running Godel supervisor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B godelctl
.B status
|
.B status
.B \-\-json
|
.B list
|
.B uptime
|
.BI start " SERVICE"
|
.BI stop " SERVICE"
|
.BI catlog " SERVICE"
|
.B reload
|
.B reboot
|
.B poweroff
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B godelctl
talks to a running Godel supervisor (PID 1) through
.IR /run/godel/ .
Every command that would signal PID 1 refuses to run when
.I /run/godel/status
is absent, so the tool is safe on dual-init machines: it will never signal
another init by accident. Never run
.B reload
under a different init.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print the status snapshot: one line per service with state, pid, restart
count, and readiness.
.TP
.B status \-\-json
Print the same snapshot as a single-line JSON array of objects with
.BR name ", " state ", " pid ", " restarts ", " ready " fields."
.TP
.B list
Alias for
.BR status .
.TP
.B uptime
Print supervisor uptime (from PID 1), service totals, summed restarts, and
the number of services whose readiness timed out.
.TP
.BI start " SERVICE"
Start a stopped service via the control FIFO.
.TP
.BI stop " SERVICE"
Stop a running service.
.TP
.BI catlog " SERVICE"
Print the service log from
.IR /var/log/godel/ .
.TP
.B reload
Ask PID 1 to re-read the configuration (SIGHUP).
.TP
.B reboot
Ask PID 1 to reboot (SIGUSR1).
.TP
.B poweroff
Ask PID 1 to power off (SIGTERM).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/godel/status
Read: status snapshot; its presence identifies a Godel boot.
.TP
.I /run/godel/ctl
Written: textual commands, one per line.
.TP
.I /var/log/godel/
Per-service logs read by
.BR catlog .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on errors and refusals.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR godel (8)
